#The program successfully implements and compares Ordinary BST and Threaded BST.

1. Both trees produce the same inorder traversal in sorted order.
2. Ordinary BST uses an auxiliary stack for inorder traversal.
3. The program measures and displays the maximum stack usage of the Ordinary BST.
4. Threaded BST performs inorder traversal without using an additional stack or recursion.
5. The program displays the predecessor and successor threads of each node, wherever present.
6. Both traversals have O(n) time complexity.
7. Ordinary BST requires O(h) auxiliary space, whereas Threaded BST requires O(1) auxiliary space for traversal.

Result:
Threaded BST provides a more space-efficient inorder traversal by using threads instead of an auxiliary stack. The program successfully demonstrates the difference in memory usage between Ordinary BST and Threaded BST.
